UNIT legacy_grade
KIND FUNCTIONAL
FIDELITY package
ANCHOR rederive@978444f3baf3 cli/verdict.mts — grade(gots, vectors) over vectorPass, threw, eq, enc and revive; the code is the truth, accidents included
ORACLE-CLASS deterministic
SIG legacy_grade(gots: list[json], vectors: list[record[name: text, expected: json]]) -> record[pass: i64, total: i64, full: bool, miss: list[text]]
ENVELOPE ABSTRACT SLICE: gots is a list of json values (the IEEE-754 specials NaN, +Infinity, -Infinity and -0 included);
  vectors is a list of records {name: text, expected: json}. A json map reaches the unit with its keys in canonical order
  (UTF-16 code units), so in this slice two maps compare by key set and values (R4) and key order is never observed. An
  expected value is the v0.4 legacy codec's stored spelling: plain json in which a map carrying the key __t is a TAG MAP
  (TABLE REVIVE). Throws are IN scope as observations: R1 says when the whole call throws.
  HOST SLICE (the js/ts extension, see NOTE): the same lists may hold host values with no language-neutral meaning — the
  absent value, a big integer, a regular expression, a boxed wrapper (number, text or bool kind), a set, a keyed
  collection, a byte array (plain or buffer-flavoured), a list with holes — and maps whose key order is significant (R4).
DEPENDS-ON none
DESCRIPTION rdv's per-vector verdict and tally for a v0.4 (legacy-codec) oracle. gots[i] is the already-computed result,
  or a captured-throw sentinel, for vectors[i]. Each vector passes when both sides are throw sentinels, or when the got,
  spelled canonically (CANON), is identical to the expected value decoded (REVIVE) and then spelled canonically. The
  result record is {pass, total, full, miss} in that field order: the passing count, the vector count, whether every
  vector passed, and the names of the first 8 failing vectors.
BEHAVIOR
R1 Vectors are graded in order, i = 0 … total-1, where total is the number of vectors; gots[i] past the end of gots is the
  absent value and gots beyond the vectors are ignored. pass = the number of vectors for which PASS(gots[i], expected) holds.
  miss = the names of the failing vectors in order, cut to the first 8 (names repeat when vectors repeat them). full =
  (pass equals total); an empty vector list gives pass 0, total 0, full true, miss empty. A throw anywhere in R5–R13 while
  grading any vector makes the WHOLE call throw (no partial result); vectors before it do not soften that.
R2 THROWN(x): x is a map (not a list, not none, not a scalar) that has an OWN key __throw. The key's value is not inspected
  (text, a number, none) and other keys may be present.
R3 PASS(g, e) = THROWN(g) and THROWN(e), or else SAME(g, e). When both are thrown, e is never decoded — so a malformed
  tag inside a thrown expected cannot throw; a thrown got against an unthrown expected falls through to SAME, and so does
  an unthrown got against a thrown expected (which is decoded, and may throw, R6).
R4 SAME(g, e): CANON(g) and CANON(REVIVE(e)) are the identical ordered tree. Lists compare element by element; maps
  compare entry by entry in KEY ORDER, keys and values both, with the same entry count; text by UTF-16 code units; finite
  numbers by value; bools; none. KEY ORDER of a map = its array-index keys (the canonical decimal spelling of an integer
  0 … 4294967294) ascending first, then every other key in arrival order. So in the host slice {a:1, b:2} is not SAME as
  {b:2, a:1}, while {b:1, "1":2} is SAME as {"1":2, b:1} CITED FIDELITY package — eq compares serialized text, ext key-order call
R5 CANON(v), applied recursively, first matching row of TABLE CANON. A tag map produced by CANON has its keys in the order
  the row lists them.
TABLE CANON — v is the value being spelled
  the absent value                                       | {__t: "undef"}
  a big integer                                          | {__t: "bigint", v: its decimal text (a leading "-" when negative)}
  the number -0                                          | {__t: "num", v: "-0"}
  NaN, +Infinity, -Infinity                               | {__t: "num", v: "NaN" / "Infinity" / "-Infinity"}
  a regular expression                                   | {__t: "regex", source: its normalized source, flags: its normalized flags} (R10)
  a boxed wrapper                                        | {__t: "boxed", k: "Number" / "String" / "Boolean" by kind, v: CANON(its payload)}
  a set                                                  | {__t: "set", values: CANON of each member in insertion order}
  a keyed collection                                     | {__t: "map", entries: [CANON(key), CANON(value)] for each entry in insertion order}
  a byte array                                           | {__t: "u8", bytes: its bytes as numbers 0–255, buf: whether it is buffer-flavoured}
  a list with a hole (an index below its length absent)   | {__t: "sparse", length: its length, entries: [index, CANON(element)] for each present index ascending}
  any other list                                         | the list of CANON of each element
  a map                                                  | the map of the same keys in KEY ORDER with CANON values; when the ORIGINAL map has an OWN key __t, __throw or __threw (any value) it is wrapped as {__t: "obj", v: that map} CITED rederive selfhost #67 — own keys only
  text, a finite number other than -0, a bool, none       | itself
R6 REVIVE(e), recursively. A list -> the list of REVIVE of each element, holes kept as holes. A map whose key __t holds
  one of the texts of TABLE REVIVE -> that row (other keys of the tag map are ignored unless the row reads them). Any other
  map (no __t, or a __t of another text or a non-text value) -> a fresh map with the same keys in the same order and REVIVE
  of each value; its own __t stays as data, so CANON wraps it again (R5) and {__t: "zzz", a: 1} is SAME as itself. Text,
  numbers, bools, none, the absent value and a big integer -> themselves. A host set, keyed collection, regular
  expression, byte array or boxed wrapper reaching REVIVE (host slice only) is read as a map of its own enumerable keys:
  empty for a set, a keyed collection, a regular expression and a boxed number or bool; a byte array gives the keys "0" …
  "n-1" holding its bytes; a boxed text gives "0" … "n-1" holding its one-character texts CITED FIDELITY package — revive's plain-map branch takes any object; ext y0–y6: an expected set equals the got {}, and a set got never equals a set expected
TABLE REVIVE — t is the tag map, t.x its key x (absent when the key is missing)
  __t "undef"   | the absent value
  __t "regex"   | a regular expression from pattern t.source and flags t.flags; an absent pattern is the empty pattern, absent flags mean no flags (R10)
  __t "bigint"  | a big integer from t.v (R11)
  __t "num"     | t.v must be exactly one of the texts "NaN", "Infinity", "-Infinity", "-0" -> that number; any other t.v (other text, a number, absent) -> throws CITED rederive selfhost #55 (round 4)
  __t "boxed"   | x = REVIVE(t.v); t.k "Number" -> boxed number TO-NUMBER(x); t.k "String" -> boxed text TO-TEXT(x); any other t.k ("Boolean", absent, anything) -> boxed bool TO-BOOL(x)
  __t "set"     | a set of REVIVE of each element of t.values in order (R8); a falsy t.values (R7) is the empty list; a t.values that is truthy and not a list -> throws
  __t "map"     | a keyed collection (R8) from t.entries read as in R9; a falsy t.entries is the empty list; truthy and not a list -> throws
  __t "u8"      | a byte array from t.bytes (R12), buffer-flavoured when t.buf is truthy (R7)
  __t "sparse"  | a list from t.length and t.entries (R13)
  __t "obj"     | t.v must be a map (none, a list, a scalar or absent -> throws, CITED rederive selfhost #55); a fresh map of t.v's keys in order with REVIVE of each value — t.v's own __t, __throw, __threw are data, never a tag
R7 TRUTHY: every value except false, 0, -0, NaN, the empty text, none and the absent value (an empty list and an empty map
  are truthy). TO-BOOL(x) = TRUTHY(x). TO-TEXT(x): text is itself; a number is its shortest round-trip digits, written
  in plain decimal notation when x is 0 or 1e-6 <= |x| < 1e21, else in scientific notation (the first digit, then "." and
  the remaining digits when there are any, then e+n or e-n): 5 -> "5", 0.5 -> "0.5", 1e21 -> "1e+21", 1.5e-7 -> "1.5e-7",
  -0 -> "0"; true/false -> "true"/"false"; none -> "null"; the absent value -> "undefined"; a list -> TO-TEXT of its
  elements joined by "," (a none or absent element gives the empty text); a map -> "[object Object]". TO-NUMBER(x): a
  number is itself; true/false -> 1/0; none -> 0; the absent value -> NaN; text -> after trimming WS from both ends, the
  empty text -> 0, a decimal literal (optional sign; digits, optionally then "." and zero or more digits, or "." then
  one or more digits; optional e/E exponent; or Infinity after the optional sign) or an unsigned 0x/0o/0b literal -> its value, anything else -> NaN; a list
  or a map -> TO-NUMBER(TO-TEXT(x)).
R8 SET MEMBERS and COLLECTION KEYS are unique under SAME-VALUE-ZERO: NaN equals NaN, -0 equals 0 (and is stored as 0),
  text/number/bool/none/big integer/absent compare by value, every list or map is distinct (REVIVE builds fresh ones). A
  later duplicate member is dropped; a later duplicate key replaces the earlier entry's value and the entry keeps its
  first position.
R9 COLLECTION ENTRIES: each element of t.entries is read as a pair by taking its first two items: a list gives its first
  and second elements (a missing one is the absent value; extra ones are ignored), a text gives its first and second
  characters (so "ab" is the pair "a", "b"); any other element (a number, none, a map, a bool) -> throws. Key and value
  are then REVIVE'd.
R10 REGULAR EXPRESSIONS: flags (when present, read as text: none is the text "null") is a set of letters from d g i m s u
  v y, each at most once, u and v not together; anything else -> throws. Normalized flags = those letters in the order
  d g i m s u v y. The pattern (read as text) must be valid in the host dialect (the js file's pin) or -> throws. Its
  normalized source: the empty pattern is "(?:)", an unescaped "/" becomes "\/", a line feed becomes the two characters
  "\n" (the host's source-escaping, the js file's pin).
R11 BIG INTEGER from t.v: text, after trimming WS from both ends — the empty text is 0; an optional sign
  then decimal digits; or (no sign) 0x/0o/0b then digits of that base; anything else -> throws. A number that is an
  integer -> that integer; a non-integer number -> throws. true/false -> 1/0. A list
  or a map -> the text rule applied to TO-TEXT of it ([5] -> 5, [] -> 0, a map -> throws). none or the absent value -> throws.
R12 BYTES from t.bytes: a list -> one byte per element, TO-NUMBER of it, NaN and ±Infinity -> 0, otherwise truncated
  toward zero and reduced modulo 256 into 0–255 (256 -> 0, -1 -> 255, 1.5 -> 1, "7" -> 7, "a" -> 0, none -> 0, true -> 1).
  A text -> when buffer-flavoured, its UTF-8 bytes ("12" -> 49, 50); otherwise one byte per character by the list rule
  ("12" -> 1, 2). A number -> the empty byte array when not buffer-flavoured, throws when buffer-flavoured. The absent value
  -> throws (either flavour).
R13 HOLED LISTS from t.length and t.entries: a t.length that is a number must be an integer in 0 … 4294967295 (else
  throws) and starts a list of that many holes; an absent t.length starts the one-element list [absent value]; any other
  t.length (text, a bool, none, a map) starts the one-element list [t.length] (not revived) CITED FIDELITY package — p2, p3, p5, v7
  Then each element of t.entries (a falsy t.entries is the empty list; a text t.entries is read as the list of its characters;
  any other truthy non-list -> throws) is a pair [i, x] read as in R9 (an element that cannot be read as a pair -> throws): i (a number or an index text like "1") puts
  REVIVE(x) at index i, extending the length when i is at or past it; an i that is not an array index (negative) puts
  nothing visible; a later pair for the same index overwrites. A list without holes then spells as a plain list (R5).
PINNED charset WS = U+0009 U+000A U+000B U+000C U+000D U+0020 U+00A0 U+1680 U+2000–U+200A U+2028 U+2029 U+202F U+205F U+3000 U+FEFF CITED R7 TO-NUMBER and R11 — the host trims exactly these
INVARIANT pass + (the number of failing vectors) = total; miss has min(8, total - pass) names; full holds exactly when
  miss is empty; no argument is mutated.
OUT-OF-DOMAIN what the code does outside ENVELOPE. Items 1–3 are reproduced in the host slice; item 4 is not.
  1. gots none with at least one vector -> throws; gots none with no vectors -> pass 0, total 0, full true, miss empty.
  2. vectors none -> throws; a vector that is none -> throws.
  3. a vector without an expected key: expected is the absent value (so it is SAME as an absent got, R1).
  4. OUT: a name that is not text (it is copied into miss as it is), gots or vectors that are maps or text (indexed by
  position), map values that are functions (CANON drops those entries), a buffer-flavoured byte array or a boxed -0,
  NaN or Infinity as a GOT (the lab's legacy argument codec cannot carry them faithfully), byte-array look-alikes whose
  storage is detached (CANON falls back to the map row), text keys of t.entries holding characters beyond U+FFFF, t.bytes that is a bool, none or a map,
  and a boxed payload (R6 boxed row) that REVIVEs to a host value.
NOTE rust is OMITTED: the unit's domain is the JS-native value model — REVIVE builds host sets, keyed collections with
  same-value-zero keys, regular expressions in the host dialect, boxed wrappers and holed lists, and R4 compares maps in
  the host's own-key order. A rust variant could only re-model those as tagged trees, and the abstract slice alone would
  verify it on a fraction of the rules. Tier 2 of REDERIVE_SELF_PLAN is js/ts.
ORACLE value -> oracles/legacy_grade.json  CANONICAL-SHA256 982a73b5342a7741c052a115ff3d12e09636d7dd0e96b1576d4564ae88ff29c1
SCOPE IN: R1–R13 over ENVELOPE (both slices) and OUT-OF-DOMAIN 1–3. OUT: OUT-OF-DOMAIN 4, the other verdict.mts exports
  (quorum, hashHex, hashOk) except as grade uses them, and the message text of any throw (not graded).
PROVENANCE rederive@978444f3baf3 (pinned authority, tree 51f9195ee1b0…), cli/verdict.mts: grade, vectorPass, threw, eq,
  enc, revive. Cut 2026-10-27 into the v0.5 contract register from the pinned source only.
